  "body": "The maintainer reported this bug in chat on 2026-11-17, on the 0.23.0 build:\n\n> serious bug; all operations are starting from World Origin.. Not WCS G54..\n\nWhat happens: each operation takes the world origin as its start or zero point. It does not take the WCS origin of the setup (work offset G54).\n\nExpected: the coordinates of each operation are measured from the WCS origin and axes of its setup. The posted program is in the work offset that `setup::wcs_slot` gives.\n\nWhy `P1`: a program with coordinates from the wrong origin cuts in the wrong place. That is wrong output (Prime Directive).\n\nNot known yet. The worker finds these first and comments them here:\n- Where the defect shows: the toolpath in the viewport, the simulation, the posted coordinates, or all three.\n- If it applies to a new setup with a moved WCS origin, or only to projects saved by an older build.\n- Which change caused it. Changes to the WCS in 0.23.0: #760 (PR #2136, Z-rotated work offset, Bake or Control), #1720 (PR #1738, WCS axis flips, project format 67), #1648 and #1926 (the Work offset control and \"Set WCS from features\" moved to the WCS page), #2210 and #2283 (a setup that holds the part turned).\n\nEvidence: none recorded yet. The first step is a test that makes a setup with a WCS origin away from the world origin, generates a 2D operation and a hole operation, and reads the toolpath and the posted X Y Z.\n\nDone means:\n- A test with a WCS origin away from the world origin pins the coordinates of the generated toolpath and of the posted program, for one 2D operation, one 3D operation and one hole operation at least.\n- The test fails on main before the correction and passes after it.\n- If a part of the defect cannot be corrected in this change, the post refuses that case by name.\n",
